The core traversal and mutation surface is small and worth knowing cold, without a framework in between:

```javascript
const list = document.querySelector('#items');

// Traversal
const first = list.firstElementChild;
const next = first.nextElementSibling;

// Mutation — each of these can trigger layout/paint work
const li = document.createElement('li');
li.textContent = 'New item';
list.appendChild(li);

// Reading computed style pulls from the CSSOM, not your stylesheet source
const styles = getComputedStyle(li);
console.log(styles.color); // resolved value, cascade already applied
```

`getComputedStyle` is the direct bridge to the CSSOM: it doesn't return what you wrote in a stylesheet, it returns the fully cascaded, inherited, resolved value for that element right now.

Reading a *layout-dependent* property (`.offsetHeight`, `.getBoundingClientRect()`) right after writing a *layout-invalidating* one (changing `.style.width`, appending a node) forces the browser to run layout **synchronously, right then**, instead of batching it for the next frame.

Do that inside a loop and you get **layout thrashing**: write, forced read, write, forced read — each iteration paying a full synchronous layout pass instead of one batched pass at the end.

```javascript
// Thrashes: write then read, repeated per element
items.forEach(el => {
  el.style.width = '100px';           // invalidates layout
  console.log(el.offsetHeight);       // forces it to resolve NOW
});

// Fixed: batch all writes, then all reads
items.forEach(el => { el.style.width = '100px'; });
items.forEach(el => { console.log(el.offsetHeight); });
```

The fix is always the same shape: separate your reads from your writes, or use `requestAnimationFrame` / a library like FastDOM to batch them automatically.
